-- #1641 CONTROL — the same interface, the same TWO impls, the same `ignore2`, the same
-- call, built with the same `build` verb. What is flipped is that the obligation is
-- DETERMINED: `zb : Bool` fixes the receiver, so there is a unique correct answer and the
-- program must build.
--
-- ⭐ HAND-DERIVED: `zb : Bool` selects `impl Zero Bool` (`zero = False`), `ignore2` discards
-- it and returns 5, so a correct compiler builds this and its binary prints `5`. Measured:
-- check 0, run `5`, build 0, binary exit 0 printing `5`.
--
-- ⚠️ WHY THIS AND NOT THE OBVIOUS ONE-DIMENSION CONTROL. The natural control is
-- plain-head-rejected.mdk — main.mdk with the wrapper removed, differing in exactly one
-- byte-level dimension. IT CANNOT BE THE GRADED CONTROL: it is CORRECTLY REJECTED at exit
-- 1, and this harness grades a control by requiring EXIT 0. Posing it as the control would
-- report CONTROL-BROKE on a perfectly healthy tree, every run, forever. It ships here
-- unGRADED instead, because it is the evidence for the claim's central comparison.
--
-- ⚠️ WHY THE WRAPPER IS ABSENT HERE, on purpose. The alternative control keeps the
-- constrained head and only determines the receiver (constrained-determined.mdk, also
-- shipped unGRADED, also measured green). That is the tighter one-dimension comparison,
-- but it would BREAK under a legitimate fix direction — rejecting a constraint wrapper on
-- an impl head outright — and a fix must never make this harness print CONTROL-BROKE's
-- advice ("the ENVIRONMENT moved, not the bug"), which would be false. This control
-- contains nothing any plausible fix for #1641 could reject, so it stays green under all
-- of them and doubles as the post-fix regression guard.
interface Zero a where
  zero : a

impl Zero Int where
  zero = 0

impl Zero Bool where
  zero = False

zb : Bool
zb = zero

ignore2 : a -> Int
ignore2 _ = 5

main = println (ignore2 zb)
